#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483496} :activity/id #uuid "7ba47f17-0fae-5ac4-aeae-fb08378b5bc9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3095f8-ebd6-5417-b5fa-228deb53654e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345465} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffb8a8a-2e10-5113-8365-30ccd72226d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345633} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9aee49-f085-5882-8939-280b36b2183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} {:answer/id #uuid "9125c680-3dee-5b40-9892-fbcf815bf7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346617} {:answer/id #uuid "f560de8e-3259-522f-ba63-0c6f8892c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3662f-50d2-5467-a302-1a748f1ec224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4dbe4152-1f13-5417-b4be-c6eaa0530f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79313" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "17a473c6-6c8d-5968-aa6a-8059d32a39f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816648e9-966e-5cf2-839c-30024fe1baea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449844} {:answer/id #uuid "5555ba44-b062-5e8b-b183-16129f0fc2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449845} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a0fd59-a9ee-584e-8be4-712573413f2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a963f06c-c986-5d91-9ccc-f663b195f77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449846} {:answer/id #uuid "f9054fa5-b0f9-5ece-93e7-1ee2ccb1796b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449846} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53ef9e0-ce91-5531-b56d-d801d314355e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0872989-936e-53ad-80f1-db5035c605ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449848} {:answer/id #uuid "355ac780-1b2e-598e-a421-8ddc9710eaf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449848} :answer-field/id #uuid "adc6cc33-91be-5597-916f-94e4653b4dba" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "34dded57-bc20-5c74-8a7c-26db7d4b2c49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674be6f-e836-53b6-ad75-f49bcd215297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482879} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdaf3df-3466-5a46-9d91-de545d17a174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482896} {:answer/id #uuid "628e0240-3161-5a31-bec3-d0d78a344f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482984} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bbb01-90f1-5914-b05b-4fdbb5c155e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483149} {:answer/id #uuid "d68f1746-c721-55d0-abfa-7820321d3544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482879} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d524d4c-bdb9-5865-a9c3-811956ada022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b149c895-74ab-53c4-8e34-ba4f249fb566" :question/math-academy-id "q-79314" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "60bb4227-9814-5e8d-9517-43a71cd69291" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f4d151-1cd0-5621-ba89-6470f4af573e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480746} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae4f21-6981-5f72-90e2-a3f32de9f3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481000} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca35bb-c3b8-5e45-a1f8-dfab2c842147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481008} {:answer/id #uuid "9c56e737-b927-5ce7-a48f-7ae0b922a01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} {:answer/id #uuid "bb990b9f-4a58-59bd-858f-c85000fc92e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481039} :answer-field/id #uuid "010bf50f-8de4-58ce-a0a7-3ef04178a4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "656e0655-2fab-5e41-bca0-28f4bd148f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35293" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "c8698e91-362f-52ce-8632-aab433d3ae0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841b51ab-4624-5135-9e62-a5d16f7f40d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118305} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5c3ff9-d114-5657-833d-571463f53ae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118306} {:answer/id #uuid "0095d96f-0e33-50d5-bec2-0593e0bb2a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118307} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd230b8-acb7-514d-8c02-14cbb3854aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} {:answer/id #uuid "43fd4f00-8337-5638-b2ff-f732db2e10d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118308} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe09fbe-4ea8-5884-b7ed-064efcb293bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3ea0ae2-1531-5d26-b98b-bdd05134f6b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34811" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ca098e19-bec0-502e-83bc-ca18e83625bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483495}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482808}]}
